✅ What Are Date-Time APIs in Java?
Date-Time APIs in Java are classes and methods provided by the Java language to handle:

Dates (like 2025-07-06)

Times (like 14:30:00)

Date-times (like 2025-07-06T14:30:00)

Time zones

Formatting and parsing

Arithmetic (add/subtract days, hours, etc.)

🔹 Two Main Date-Time APIs in Java
| API                                    | Description                                      |
| -------------------------------------- | ------------------------------------------------ |
| `java.util.Date`, `java.util.Calendar` | **Old**, error-prone API (pre-Java 8)            |
| `java.time.*` (Java 8+)                | **Modern**, powerful, thread-safe, and preferred |


🔸 1. Old API (Before Java 8)
java.util.Date

java.util.Calendar

java.text.SimpleDateFormat

❌ These are mutable and not thread-safe, and are not recommended for new applications.

🔸 2. New API: java.time Package (Java 8+)
Introduced in Java 8 as part of JSR-310, inspired by Joda-Time, and now the standard.

✅ Key Classes:
| Class               | Purpose                                   |
| ------------------- | ----------------------------------------- |
| `LocalDate`         | Date only (no time)                       |
| `LocalTime`         | Time only (no date)                       |
| `LocalDateTime`     | Both date and time (no time zone)         |
| `ZonedDateTime`     | Date-time with time zone                  |
| `Instant`           | Machine timestamp                         |
| `Duration`          | Time-based amount (seconds, millis, etc.) |
| `Period`            | Date-based amount (years, months, days)   |
| `DateTimeFormatter` | Formatting and parsing                    |


🧪 Examples
✅ LocalDate (just date)

LocalDate today = LocalDate.now();
LocalDate birthday = LocalDate.of(1995, 5, 23);
✅ LocalTime (just time)

LocalTime now = LocalTime.now();
✅ LocalDateTime (date + time)

LocalDateTime current = LocalDateTime.now();
✅ ZonedDateTime (date + time + time zone)

ZonedDateTime zdt = ZonedDateTime.now(ZoneId.of("Asia/Karachi"));
✅ Formatting Date

DateTimeFormatter formatter = DateTimeFormatter.ofPattern("dd-MM-yyyy");
String formatted = LocalDate.now().format(formatter);
🆚 Why Prefer java.time API?
| Old API (`Date`, `Calendar`) | New API (`java.time`)    |
| ---------------------------- | ------------------------ |
| Not thread-safe              | Thread-safe              |
| Hard to read/write/format    | Clean and fluent         |
| Legacy and confusing         | Modern and well-designed |
